2. Parallel gripper: SO-ARM-102 by Robonine (CC BY 4.0 / CERN-OHL-P-2.0)

The simulated gripper's opening, stroke and frame size, and the "stock" finger
entrant, were measured from the open-source SO-ARM-102 parallel gripper: Robonine,
SO-ARM 102, https://github.com/roboninecom/SO-ARM-102, commit
190eb162c9e59d7f6dceab850ea1b5c6a4539c14. Copyright notice as supplied upstream:
Copyright (c) 2026 Robonine. Per that project's LICENSING.md and REUSE.toml, its
simulation files (simulation/, including the URDF meshes) are licensed CC BY 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), its hardware sources (models/: STEP,
STL, 3MF) CERN-OHL-P-2.0 (https://ohwr.org/cern_ohl_p_v2.txt), and its software
Apache-2.0.

v1 (14-box fingers): the stock entrant is our own box approximation of its finger, and
the printable jaw in the print pack is drawn from the documented interface dimensions
only. No files from that project are used.

v2 (part-shaped fingers): three of the gripper's CC BY 4.0 simulation meshes
(simulation/urdf/meshes/link5_1.stl, clamp1_1.stl and clamp2_1.stl) are vendored,
unmodified, in assets/gripper/so_arm_102/meshes/, with the licence text in
LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0.txt, their source in SOURCE.md and SHA-256 pins in lock.json
(scripts/fetch_assets.py fetches and verifies them). They are drawn as visual-only
geometry, and the v2 stock entrant is the gripper's own blade from those meshes. Files
derived from them that this repository commits or publishes (the stock blade's convex
hulls, and the GLB meshes of the gripper frame, carriage and blade used by the web app
and renders) are adaptations under CC BY 4.0. Modified: cut at the carriage interface,
re-posed, simplified, split into convex pieces (CoACD, threshold 0.02, seed 0),
re-encoded as GLB. The scored simulation never collides with these meshes except the
stock blade's hulls, which are the stock entrant. If a print pack ever includes a clamp
made from the project's CERN-OHL-P-2.0 STL files, that file will carry the
CERN-OHL-P-2.0 notice and a statement of the modifications, as the licence requires.


3. NVIDIA Nemotron models (used through an API)

Designs, hypotheses and agent turns are generated by NVIDIA Nemotron 3 models
(nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b and nvidia/Nemotron-3_5-Lightning), served by
Nebius Token Factory. No model weights are included. Recorded model
outputs (response caches, session recordings) are part of the results. The models'
own licences and terms are on their model cards.


